{"book":23,"chapter":14,"context":{"chapter":14,"title":"How Art Thou Fallen","summary":"Israel will be restored and will mock the fallen king of Babylon, whose arrival among the dead stirs the ghosts of other kings. Short oracles against Assyria and Philistia affirm that God's purpose cannot be overturned.","background":"Verses 12–15 picture a king who tried to climb above the stars and was flung down. 'Lucifer' is the Latin Vulgate's word for the Hebrew helel, 'shining one' or morning star. Many early Christian writers applied the passage to Satan's fall; in context it taunts a human king of Babylon, and interpreters still discuss whether it points beyond him.","customs":[{"term":"The sides of the north","verses":"14:13","note":"In Canaanite myth the gods met on a sacred mountain in the far north. The king wanted a seat among them."}],"watchFor":["Dead kings greet him: 'Art thou also become weak as we?' (v. 10).","Five 'I will' boasts (vv. 13–14) end in 'Yet thou shalt be brought down' (v. 15).","The Philistine oracle is dated to the year King Ahaz died (v. 28)."],"connections":["Ezekiel 28:12-17","Luke 10:18","Psalms 48:2"]},"verses":{"1":{"w":[["כִּי","ki","H3588","HTc","for"],["יְרַחֵם","yerachem","H7355","HVpi3ms","he will have compassion on"],["יְהוָה","yahweh","H3068","HNpt","Yahweh"],["אֶת","'et-","H853","HTo","<obj.>"],["יַעֲקֹב","ya'ako","H3290","HNpm","Jacob"],["וּבָחַר","uvachar","H977","Hc/Vqq3ms","and he will choose"],["עוֹד","'od","H5750","HAcmsa","again"],["בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל","beyisra'el","H3478","HR/Npl","<in> Israel"],["וְהִנִּיחָם","vehinicham","H5117","Hc/Vhq3ms/Sp3mp","and he will give rest them"],["עַל","'al-","H5921","HR","on"],["אַדְמָתָם","'admatam","H127","HNcfsc/Sp3mp","own land their"],["וְנִלְוָה","venilvah","H3867","Hc/VNq3ms","and he will join himself"],["הַגֵּר","hager","H1616","HTd/Ncmsa","the sojourner"],["עֲלֵיהֶם","'aleihem","H5921","HR/Sp3mp","to them"],["וְנִסְפְּחוּ","venispechu","H5596","Hc/VNq3cp","and they will attach themselve